              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The whitish film that develops on your glass after about a week is incredibly common. Even though you are using filtered water and only putting water in the glass, there are two primary culprits behind this film: **mineral buildup (limescale)** and **biofilm (microbial growth)**. \n\nThe exact cause depends on what the film feels like and how easily it comes off.\n\n---\n\n### Culprit 1: Mineral Buildup (Limescale)\nEven though your water comes from a fridge filter, standard refrigerator filters use **activated carbon**. While carbon is excellent at removing chlorine, odors, bad tastes, and some contaminants, it **does not remove dissolved hard water minerals** like calcium and magnesium. \n\n* **How it forms:** Every time you take a sip or pour water out of the glass, a microscopic layer of water is left clinging to the inner walls of the glass. This water quickly evaporates, leaving behind those dissolved calcium and magnesium minerals. Over the course of a week, as you repeatedly refill and drink from the glass, these minerals layer on top of each other, building up into a visible, cloudy, whitish film.\n* **What it looks and feels like:** It is usually dry, slightly chalky or rough to the touch, and will not easily wipe away with just water or dry rubbing.\n\n### Culprit 2: Biofilm (Bacterial Growth)\nIf you are reusing the same glass for a week without washing it with soap, you are inadvertently creating a miniature ecosystem.\n\n* **How it forms:** Every time your lips touch the glass, you transfer saliva, skin cells, natural skin oils, and mouth bacteria into the cup. Even when the cup is empty, invisible microbes from the air settle into it. Because the glass remains consistently moist over a week, these bacteria feed on the organic matter from your mouth and begin to multiply. They bind themselves to the glass and secrete a sticky, protective substance, creating a thin, cloudy layer known as a **biofilm**.\n* **What it looks and feels like:** It often looks foggy or hazy. When the glass is wet, the film will feel slightly slippery, slimy, or \"scummy\". \n\n---\n\n### How to Tell the Difference (The \"Vinegar Test\")\nTo easily figure out which one you are dealing with (or if it's a combination of both), try the **Vinegar Swipe**:\n1. Take a cloth or paper towel and dampen it with plain white vinegar.\n2. Rub the white film on the glass.\n3. **If the film dissolves instantly:** It is **mineral buildup**. The acid in the vinegar chemically breaks down the alkaline calcium deposits.\n4. **If the vinegar does nothing, but warm soapy water wipes it away:** It is **biofilm**. \n\n### How to Prevent and Clean It\n* **To remove mineral buildup:** Soak the glass in a 50/50 mix of warm water and white vinegar for 15 to 30 minutes, then wash normally. \n* **To prevent biofilm:** You should wash your drinking glass with **dish soap, warm water, and a sponge** every 1 to 2 days. Simply rinsing the glass with water is not enough, as biofilms are physically glued to the glass and require the friction of a sponge or brush to be broken up. \n* **If you want to eliminate minerals entirely:** Standard pitcher and fridge filters won't remove calcium, but a **Reverse Osmosis (RO) system** or an **ion-exchange filter** (such as a ZeroWater filter) will strip all dissolved minerals, preventing limescale film from ever forming.",
